# Podman DNS Bypass Pattern

Workaround for the Podman 5.x `--dns` append-not-override quirk, used when a
container **must not** resolve through AdGuardHome.

Read `docs/DNS_ARCHITECTURE.md` in the repo root for the full narrative. For
the related `<service>.container-manual` reference-file convention, see the
sister rule `dual-quadlet-pattern.mdc`.

## The Podman `--dns` append-not-override quirk

Podman 5.4.1 (verified on aevion) **appends** `--dns` values to
`containers.conf` `dns_servers` instead of overriding them. This contradicts
the Podman manpage which says `--dns` overrides.

Proof (run on aevion):

```bash
# containers.conf has dns_servers = ["192.168.1.105"]
podman run --rm --dns 1.1.1.1 alpine cat /etc/resolv.conf
# nameserver 192.168.1.105
# nameserver 1.1.1.1        ← both appear

CONTAINERS_CONF=/dev/null podman run --rm --dns 1.1.1.1 alpine cat /etc/resolv.conf
# nameserver 1.1.1.1        ← ONLY this, as desired
```

This matters especially for **Alpine/musl-based images** (most LinuxServer
images, AdGuard, etc.). Musl's resolver queries ALL nameservers in parallel;
whichever responds first wins. So a container whose resolv.conf has both AGH
(`192.168.1.105`) and a public resolver (`1.1.1.1`) is in a non-deterministic
race — AGH's sinkhole IP for a blocked domain can beat a legitimate answer
from `1.1.1.1`.

## When to use the bypass

Apply the `CONTAINERS_CONF=/dev/null` bypass when **both** are true:

1. The container must NOT use AGH (tracker false positives, AGH's own
   bootstrap preventing self-reference, etc.).
2. The image's libc behavior (musl, or glibc with parallel queries) makes
   having AGH anywhere in resolv.conf a correctness risk.

Do NOT apply it "just in case". Every service that should be filtered by AGH
must stay on the default (`dns_servers` from `podman/containers.conf`).

## How to apply the bypass (three required pieces)

In the service's `podman-setup.sh`:

```bash
# 1) Prefix the podman run invocation with the env override. This makes the
#    INITIAL container created during install have the right resolv.conf.
CONTAINERS_CONF=/dev/null podman run \
 ...
 --dns 1.1.1.1 \
 --dns 9.9.9.9 \
 ...

# 2) After podlet generates the quadlet, inject the same env var into the
#    [Service] section so SUBSEQUENT systemd restarts apply it too.
action_based_on_query "generate-con-quadlet" "$NAME"
action_based_on_query "inject-quadlet-env" "$NAME" "Environment=CONTAINERS_CONF=/dev/null"
action_based_on_query "install-con-quadlet" "$NAME" "$NAME"
```

The `inject-quadlet-env` action is defined in `scripts/podman/container.sh`
(`inject_env_into_quadlet`). It is idempotent (re-running `podman-setup.sh`
won't duplicate the line) and uses `awk` for portability.

3) Document the bypass inline with a pointer to `docs/DNS_ARCHITECTURE.md`.

## Because this is a quadlet-level directive, also add a manual reference file

Any service using this bypass MUST also have a `<service>.container-manual`
file committed alongside the auto-generated `<service>.container` — because
the `Environment=CONTAINERS_CONF=/dev/null` line is injected post-generation
and is easy to miss in diffs. See `dual-quadlet-pattern.mdc`.

## Related code paths

- `scripts/podman/container.sh` → `inject_env_into_quadlet()`
- `scripts/common.sh` → case `"inject-quadlet-env")`
- `podman/containers.conf` → global `dns_servers`
- `apps/qbittorrent/podman-setup.sh` → reference implementation (AGH-filtered trackers)
- `apps/adguardhome/podman-setup.sh` → reference implementation (self-reference avoidance)
- `docs/DNS_ARCHITECTURE.md` → full story
